course: dictionary
lesson: 5247
title: Official words and softer words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are chosen to sound kinder or more polite than a plain word. Governments and job centres often do this. The plain word is «unemployed», which means you have no job. «Jobseeker» sounds more positive, but in its main sense it means the same thing: someone with no job who is looking for one.
explain: Read the context clue carefully. «"Jobseeker" means an unemployed person.» The sentence tells you the meaning straight away, because it explains the word for you. When a word is explained in its own sentence, look for clue words such as means, is or stands for.
explain: Jobseeker has a second, wider meaning too. Anyone who is looking for a job is a jobseeker, even if they already have one. So a person with a job who is searching for a better one is also a jobseeker. Always check which meaning fits the sentence you are reading.
explain: The word is mostly used in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand. In the UK, Jobseeker's Allowance is the name of a benefit, a payment from the state. In Australia the name is JobSeeker Payment. When you meet a term from another country, check where it is used.
fact: Jobseeker means a person who is looking for work.
fact: «Jobseeker» means an unemployed person, especially one getting state benefits.
fact: A euphemism is a softer or more polite word used in place of a plainer one, and «jobseeker» is one.
fact: In its second sense, a jobseeker is anyone looking for a job, even if they already have one.
fact: Jobseeker's Allowance (UK) and JobSeeker Payment (Australia) are the names of benefits.
exercise: «"Jobseeker" means an unemployed person.» What does jobseeker mean in this sentence? | A shop worker who loves the job | *An unemployed person, someone with no job | A teacher who works at a school | A person who runs a job centre
exercise: Mia has a job at the shop, but she searches online for a better one. Is she a jobseeker? | Yes, but only in Australia | *Yes, anyone looking for a job is a jobseeker | No, only people with no job at all | No, jobseekers must be children
exercise: A government says «jobseeker» instead of «unemployed». Why might it do this? | It is a longer word that sounds clever | *It sounds more positive, a softer way to say it | It means a person who owns a shop | It is only used in Scotland
test: Which word is the softer, more official way to say «unemployed»? | Benefit | Jobless | *Jobseeker | Lazy
test: Where is the word jobseeker mostly used? | Only in France | *The UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand | Only on the moon | Equally in every country in the world
exam: Which sentence uses jobseeker in its wider sense? | Sam is a jobseeker, so he is sure to get a benefit | *Sam has a job, but he is still a jobseeker, always searching for something better | Sam is a jobseeker because he is a builder | Sam works at the job centre as a jobseeker
